1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,479
Aarav's phone has a chip with
billions of tiny switches.

2
00:00:03,779 --> 00:00:06,723
Each switch takes almost
no current at its control.

3
00:00:07,023 --> 00:00:11,449
In fact, its control gate does not
even touch the part it controls.

4
00:00:11,749 --> 00:00:13,387
This switch is a MOSFET.

5
00:00:13,387 --> 00:00:14,931
Let's see how it works.

6
00:00:15,431 --> 00:00:17,645
Put a magnet under a glass table.

7
00:00:17,995 --> 00:00:20,929
Iron dust on top lines up into a path.

8
00:00:21,279 --> 00:00:23,641
The magnet never touches the dust.

9
00:00:23,641 --> 00:00:25,431
The glass is in between.

10
00:00:25,781 --> 00:00:28,458
Take the magnet away,
and the path breaks up.

11
00:00:29,108 --> 00:00:30,854
A MOSFET does the same with electrons.

12
00:00:31,204 --> 00:00:35,348
Its gate is a metal plate,
sitting on a thin layer of glass.

13
00:00:35,698 --> 00:00:37,781
That glass is silicon dioxide.

14
00:00:37,781 --> 00:00:39,711
It lets no current through.

15
00:00:40,061 --> 00:00:43,959
So the gate pulls electrons by
its voltage alone, not by current.

16
00:00:44,609 --> 00:00:47,482
Let's build an n channel
enhancement MOSFET.

17
00:00:47,832 --> 00:00:50,302
Start with a block of p type silicon.

18
00:00:50,302 --> 00:00:51,824
This is the substrate.

19
00:00:52,174 --> 00:00:53,842
Add two n type islands.

20
00:00:53,842 --> 00:00:56,423
One is the source, one is the drain.

21
00:00:56,773 --> 00:01:00,149
Cover the top with a thin
layer of silicon dioxide.

22
00:01:00,499 --> 00:01:02,426
Put a metal gate on top.

23
00:01:02,426 --> 00:01:04,588
Metal, oxide, semiconductor.

24
00:01:04,938 --> 00:01:07,225
That is where the name comes from.

25
00:01:07,225 --> 00:01:08,125
MOS.

26
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:12,118
Notice, between source and drain,
there is no channel at all.

27
00:01:12,768 --> 00:01:13,927
Now the breadboard.

28
00:01:13,927 --> 00:01:16,219
A lamp connects the drain to the supply.

29
00:01:16,569 --> 00:01:18,004
Gate at zero volts.

30
00:01:18,004 --> 00:01:19,947
No channel, so no current.

31
00:01:19,947 --> 00:01:21,232
The lamp is off.

32
00:01:21,582 --> 00:01:23,566
Raise the gate to one volt.

33
00:01:23,566 --> 00:01:24,649
Still nothing.

34
00:01:24,999 --> 00:01:28,330
At two volts, the gate pulls
enough electrons under the oxide.

35
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:30,562
They form a thin n channel.

36
00:01:30,562 --> 00:01:32,587
We call it an induced channel.

37
00:01:32,937 --> 00:01:34,195
Current starts.

38
00:01:34,195 --> 00:01:37,889
This gate voltage is the
threshold voltage, VT.

39
00:01:38,239 --> 00:01:39,701
Raise the gate more.

40
00:01:39,701 --> 00:01:41,457
The channel grows thicker.

41
00:01:41,457 --> 00:01:42,811
More current flows.

42
00:01:43,161 --> 00:01:45,678
The gate builds, or enhances, the channel.

43
00:01:45,678 --> 00:01:47,509
So it is an enhancement MOSFET.

44
00:01:47,859 --> 00:01:49,950
At zero gate volts it is off.

45
00:01:49,950 --> 00:01:51,884
So we call it normally off.

46
00:01:52,534 --> 00:01:54,266
Now the depletion MOSFET.

47
00:01:54,616 --> 00:01:57,718
It is made with a thin n
channel already built in.

48
00:01:58,068 --> 00:02:01,390
So at zero gate volts,
current already flows.

49
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:03,270
Make the gate negative.

50
00:02:03,270 --> 00:02:04,894
It pushes electrons away.

51
00:02:04,894 --> 00:02:06,135
The channel thins.

52
00:02:06,485 --> 00:02:08,155
This is depletion mode.

53
00:02:08,155 --> 00:02:09,509
The current falls.

54
00:02:09,859 --> 00:02:11,343
Make the gate positive.

55
00:02:11,343 --> 00:02:13,061
It pulls in more electrons.

56
00:02:13,061 --> 00:02:14,264
The current rises.

57
00:02:14,614 --> 00:02:16,479
This is enhancement mode.

58
00:02:16,479 --> 00:02:19,008
A depletion MOSFET works both ways.

59
00:02:19,658 --> 00:02:21,459
Compare their transfer curves.

60
00:02:21,809 --> 00:02:25,217
The enhancement type
stays at zero until VT.

61
00:02:25,217 --> 00:02:26,483
Then it rises.

62
00:02:26,833 --> 00:02:31,220
The depletion type already
has IDSS at zero gate volts.

63
00:02:31,570 --> 00:02:36,165
It falls to zero at VGS off,
and rises for positive gates.

64
00:02:36,515 --> 00:02:40,042
In the symbol, a broken
channel line means enhancement.

65
00:02:40,042 --> 00:02:41,185
No channel yet.

66
00:02:41,535 --> 00:02:43,465
A solid line means depletion.

67
00:02:43,465 --> 00:02:45,394
The channel is already there.

68
00:02:46,044 --> 00:02:48,529
Depletion is on until you squeeze it.

69
00:02:48,529 --> 00:02:51,063
Enhancement is off until you build it.

70
00:02:51,863 --> 00:02:54,114
Now the maths, one step at a time.

71
00:02:54,464 --> 00:02:58,417
For the enhancement type,
below VT, ID is zero.

72
00:02:59,117 --> 00:03:04,423
Above VT, ID equals k,
times VGS minus VT, squared.

73
00:03:05,123 --> 00:03:07,044
k is a constant for each device.

74
00:03:07,044 --> 00:03:09,389
Its unit is milliamperes per volt squared.

75
00:03:10,089 --> 00:03:14,259
For the depletion type, use
Shockley's equation, like the JFET.

76
00:03:14,959 --> 00:03:21,154
ID equals IDSS, times one minus
VGS over VGS off, squared.

77
00:03:21,854 --> 00:03:24,672
And it now works for
positive gate voltages too.

78
00:03:25,322 --> 00:03:26,495
Worked example.

79
00:03:26,495 --> 00:03:29,574
An enhancement MOSFET has VT of two volts.

80
00:03:29,924 --> 00:03:32,880
Its k is zero point five
milliamperes per volt squared.

81
00:03:33,230 --> 00:03:35,946
Find ID when the gate is at five volts.

82
00:03:36,296 --> 00:03:37,196
First check.

83
00:03:37,175 --> 00:03:38,814
Five volts is above VT.

84
00:03:38,814 --> 00:03:40,254
So the channel exists.

85
00:03:40,954 --> 00:03:43,646
VGS minus VT is three volts.

86
00:03:44,346 --> 00:03:45,793
Three squared is nine.

87
00:03:46,493 --> 00:03:48,709
ID is zero point five times nine.

88
00:03:48,709 --> 00:03:50,478
Four point five milliamperes.

89
00:03:51,128 --> 00:03:52,590
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:03:52,940 --> 00:03:56,736
Many students think every MOSFET
conducts at zero gate volts.

91
00:03:57,086 --> 00:03:59,078
Only the depletion type does.

92
00:03:59,078 --> 00:04:01,537
The enhancement type is off at zero.

93
00:04:02,187 --> 00:04:03,972
And handle MOSFETs with care.

94
00:04:03,972 --> 00:04:07,042
Static from your fingers
can puncture the thin oxide.

95
00:04:07,392 --> 00:04:09,013
Where is this asked?

96
00:04:09,013 --> 00:04:10,873
In PUC board papers.

97
00:04:11,523 --> 00:04:16,464
Name the types of FET, compare the
MOSFET types, and draw their symbols.

98
00:04:17,114 --> 00:04:18,014
Try this.

99
00:04:17,889 --> 00:04:22,299
A depletion MOSFET has IDSS of
eight milliamperes, and VGS off of

100
00:04:22,299 --> 00:04:23,508
minus four volts.

101
00:04:23,508 --> 00:04:26,445
Find ID when the gate
is at plus two volts.

102
00:04:31,845 --> 00:04:32,745
D.

103
00:04:32,099 --> 00:04:33,008
Eighteen milliamperes.

104
00:04:33,358 --> 00:04:34,759
Use Shockley's equation.

105
00:04:34,759 --> 00:04:37,093
Two over minus four is minus one half.

106
00:04:37,443 --> 00:04:40,574
One minus minus one
half is one point five.

107
00:04:40,924 --> 00:04:43,695
One point five squared
is two point two five.

108
00:04:44,045 --> 00:04:47,369
Eight times two point two
five is eighteen milliamperes.

109
00:04:47,369 --> 00:04:50,620
More than IDSS, because
it is enhancement mode.

110
00:04:51,270 --> 00:04:52,816
Screenshot this card.

111
00:04:56,016 --> 00:04:57,714
Next, the DC load line.

112
00:04:57,714 --> 00:05:00,713
Where should a transistor
sit to amplify cleanly?
